{
 "about": "Our fluxatl telluric correction against Kurucz's own, scan by scan, 2026-10-25: the flatness score (scripts/score_telluric_correction.py --authors kurucz2005 --relative-transmission; scan 109 with --telluric-below 0.99) of our corrected spectrum and of Kurucz's 2005 residual irradiance atlas (irradrelwl.dat, kurucz.harvard.edu/sun/irradiance2005; read_kurucz_irradiance), on the same pixels. Kurucz's is aligned per page where nothing absorbs (-0.10 to -0.13 km/s median) and its hand-interpolated straight stretches are left out for both, which favours it. 'native' scores ours as fitted; 'smoothed' gives ours Kurucz's Gaussian first (--smooth-fwhm-cm1 = Table 1's smoothed resolving power: 0.0714, 0.0545, 0.0426 cm-1 for scans 109, 111, 113), since his release was smoothed at three times each scan's dispersion. Scan 115 (1000-1300 nm) was never re-reduced: Kurucz's spectrum stops at 1001 nm.",
 "scans": {
  "109": {
   "native": {
    "method": "niratl ships the authors' own corrected solar spectrum (Wallace, Hinkle & Livingston 1993). Ours minus theirs mixes both corrections' errors, so instead each is scored on its own, the same way: at pixels where the model transmission is below 0.97 and the solar model is flat (above 0.995 of its continuum), the rms of the corrected spectrum about a running mean of nearby pixels with neither solar nor telluric absorption. A perfect correction leaves noise there. The first and last 50 pixels are not scored: an edge pixel is inside the neighbouring page too (pages overlap by 5 cm-1), and a fit that leaves edges out should not be judged on them. Nor are pixels below a transmission of 0.15, which the corrected products blank: dividing by a saturated core turns a percent of model error into a spike that can outweigh the rest of a window. Two bins: every telluric pixel (T < 0.97) and the deep ones (T < 0.7).",
    "settings": {
     "stem": "fluxatl_109",
     "authors": "kurucz2005",
     "relative_transmission": true,
     "telluric_below": 0.99
    },
